# [Repository Name] Axioms

These are not theses. Axioms are the universal constraints that apply to
every thesis, master plan, and project in this domain. They cannot be
falsified and need not be managed — but they should be read before forming
any thesis, because every thesis operates within these boundaries.

The difference between an axiom and a thesis: a thesis has a
"What Would Change My Mind" section. An axiom does not. It holds
regardless of what you observe or believe.

---

## The Entropy Law

All edge evaporates. Every exploitable inefficiency attracts capital,
attention, or imitation until the inefficiency is priced away. The
question is never "will this edge disappear?" — it will. The questions
are: how fast, what are the early warning signs, and what comes after?

This is not pessimism. A finite window of edge, well-exploited, is
sufficient. The planning implication: every thesis must name its decay
mechanism and its horizon. Every master plan must name what it does
when the window closes.

## The Observer Effect

Systematic exploitation of a pattern changes the pattern. A signal
that becomes widely known and acted upon loses its statistical properties —
not because the underlying reality changed, but because the act of
extraction is part of the reality. Secrecy and speed are the only
defenses, and both are temporary.

## Reflexivity

Markets are partially constituted by participant beliefs. A thesis acted
on at scale changes the conditions that made it true. No model is immune
to its own success. The most dangerous moment for any strategy is when
it begins to work well enough that others notice.

## Finite Precision

All signals have noise floors. Beyond a certain resolution, the marginal
value of prediction approaches zero while costs — data, compute,
complexity — approach infinity. Knowing when to stop refining is as
important as knowing how to refine.

---

*These axioms are assumed, not proven. Any foundation is ultimately
assumed — the alternative is infinite regress, which produces only
paralysis and entropy. We stop here not because this is truth, but
because continuing produces no decisions. Decisions are the point.
Without them, only the cold remains.*
